 ## Overview
-From-Region Visibility Preprocessing is a technique that precomputes visibility information for specific regions of a scene to optimize rendering performance. It solves the problem of determining which geometry is visible from a given viewpoint or region before the rendering pass, allowing for the culling of occluded or irrelevant geometry. It is distinguished from its siblings by its focus on precomputing visibility for *regions* rather than individual points or static blocks, often involving complex occluder fusion and significant preprocessing overhead.
+From-region visibility preprocessing is a technique that precomputes the set of visible geometry for specific viewing regions or viewpoints to accelerate rendering. It solves the problem of expensive per-frame visibility testing for large datasets by shifting the computational burden to an offline phase. Unlike [Face Culling](8735-face-culling.md) which operates on local voxel adjacency, this method considers global occlusion relationships, often involving complex occluder fusion, but historically suffers from significant preprocessing overhead.
 
 ## How it works
